{
  "id": "Compound::DB00376 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Trihexyphenidyl, a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, is shown to bind to Chrm4 and Chrm5, both of which are involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity. This activity is ultimately linked to the participation of Chrm3, suggesting a potential role for Trihexyphenidyl in modulating the function of muscarinic receptors. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Trihexyphenidyl may influence Chrm3 through a shared pathway involving muscarinic receptor activation. The consistent binding to Chrm4 and Chrm5, along with their participation in the same functional pathway, supports a mechanism where Trihexyphenidyl could modulate Chrm3 activity by inhibiting muscarinic receptor function. The integrated evidence suggests that Trihexyphenidyl may exert its effects on Chrm3 through a mechanism involving muscarinic receptor signaling, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
